'''''Stenocactus multicostatus''''', the '''brain cactus''', is a member of the [[Cactaceae|cactus family]] native to the deserts of Mexico, and is popular in the gardening community. It has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/327544/i-stenocactus-multicostatus-i/details |title=''Stenocactus multicostatus'' |publisher=Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=18 May 2023 }}</ref>

==Description==
It grows  single a plant body that is spherical to somewhat cylindrical up to {{cvt|6|cm}} tall and {{cvt|6|–|10|cm}} wide. It has no fewer than 120 very thin, sharp-edged, pressed, wavy ribs, between which there are narrow furrows. It has nearly 100 thin ribs around the outside, each with six to nine spines. Three white, papery, curved central spines arise from the areole, which are up to 3 centimeters long and have a square cross-section. The four marginal spines are glassy white, upright or slightly curved. In some plants there can be as many as 144 ribs, with the highest [[phyllotaxis]] (55/144) of any cactus.<ref>{{cite book | last= Borg M.D. | first= Prof. J. | date= 1937 | title= Cacti | location= London | publisher= MacMillan and Co. | page= 223}}</ref> The crown is covered by a thin, whitish wool. The upper spines are wider, at {{cvt|4|–|8|cm}} long, while the lower ones are shorter, at {{cvt|5|–|15|mm}} long. The flower is white to purple, {{cvt|2|–|5|cm}} diameter with many stamens. The scales of the ovary are broadly ovoid, pointed to pointed, very thin, more or less papery and fall off early.<ref>{{cite web| title=Brain Cactus| publisher=Desert Tropicals| access-date=2015-01-11| url=http://www.desert-tropicals.com/Plants/Cactaceae/Stenocactus_multicostatus.html}}</ref>
